w generative model.","evidence_snippet":"“In this review, we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics…”"},{"record_id":"rec_003258","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title points to a biomedical foundation model, but the abstract reads like a proposal ('Here I propose to work on...'). That is not enough to confirm a completed primary model paper or foundation-model details.","evidence_snippet":"“Foundation model for biomedical graphs…”; “Here I propose to work on multimodal LLM … foundation model”"},{"record_id":"rec_003259","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The fragment links LLMs to biological sequence analysis, but it is too incomplete to determine whether this is a primary study, review, or resource. The model and foundation-model status cannot be resolved confidently from the abstract alone.","evidence_snippet":"“Large language models for biological sequence analysis in infectious disease research”"},{"record_id":"rec_003261","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly says 'a survey,' and the abstract says it comprehensively reviews LLMs in biomedical data analysis. No new generative model is described.","evidence_snippet":"“The large language models on biomedical data analysis: a survey”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0211.stderr.log:547:./data/fulltext_screening_context_2026-07-09_compact_v2/fulltext_screening_input.json:13513:      "full_text_context": "[abstract: Abstract]\nTransformer model has been a de-facto standard in natural language processing. Its adaptations in other fields such as computer vision showed promising results that this architecture is a powerful neural network in representation learning regardless of the data type. This recent success has led to research in multimodal Large Language Model (LLM), which enabled us to new types of tasks and applications with multiple data types. However, multimodal LLM in the biomedical domain is primarily limited to images, text, and/or sequence data. Here I propose to work on multimodal LLM architecture for biomedical graphs such as protein structure and chemical molecules. The research hypothesis is based on the fact that clinicians and researchers in computational biology and clinical research take advantage of various information for their decision-making process. Therefore, an AI model being able to handle multiple data types should boost its ability to use diverse knowledge for improved performances in clinical applications.\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nThe foundation model revolutionized not only natural language processing (NLP) but also the humanAI interaction after the release of ChatGPT service by OpenAI (OpenAI, 2023a). ChatGPT enhanced the usability with a chat interface allowing users to instruct large language model (LLM) for any tasks even the ones requiring complex domain knowledge such as medical domain text (Savage et al., 2024). The emergence of open-source medical LLMs has further enhanced access to these technologies in healthcare settings, addressing privacy concerns associated with patient data (Toma et al., 2023; Kweon et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2023).\n\nThis success of the foundation model quickly extended to computer vision (CV), expanding the application of chat assistant tools to medical image analytics (OpenAI, 2023b; Li et al., 2023b;\n\nTu et al., 2023). Recently, visual instruction tuning was introduced to open the possibility of a visual assistant in medicine (Li et al., 2023a; Lee et al., 2023). Additionally, there has been notable progress in extending the model's capabilities to handle biological sequences, including DNA sequences and chemical sequences represented by Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry Specification (SMILES) notation (Taylor et al., 2022; Consens et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2024).\n\nDespite these advancements, multimodal research in biomedicine has focused on integrating text, image, and sequence data. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nThis proposal describes the plan to develop a foundation model architecture uniquely trained to understand the complexities of biomedical graphs. Unlike existing models that primarily focus on text, images, or sequences, the proposed model aims to bridge the gap by integrating information from diverse data types such as knowledge graphs, protein structures, and chemical molecules. By leveraging the strengths of large language models in capturing textual information and combining them with specialized encoders for biological sequences and graph structures, the foundation model holds immense potential to revolutionize various aspects of healthcare, including diagnosis, treatment planning, and drug discovery.\n\nThe model can be used to create an interactive agent that clinicians and researchers can utilize to help them navigate problems in biomedical research, thereby enhancing decision-making processes in clinical practice and computational biology research. For instance, the incorporation of knowledge graphs may allow for a more nuanced exploration of relationships between genes, drugs, and diseases, facilitating target identification for drug discovery as well as drug repurposing, which accelerates the clinical trial progress.\n\nMoreover, the integration of protein structure and chemical molecule data should enable our model to delve deeper into molecular mechanisms underlying diseases and drug interactions. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Methods]\nThe proposed foundation model for biomedical graphs, depicted in Figure 1, integrates multiple specialized encoders with a backbone LLM that is also trained for the biomedical domain to effectively process and understand diverse biomedical data types such as medical images and ontologies.",
./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0211.stderr.log:548:./data/fulltext_screening_context_2026-07-09_compact_v2/fulltext_screening_input.json:13539:          "text": "This proposal describes the plan to develop a foundation model architecture uniquely trained to understand the complexities of biomedical graphs. Unlike existing models that primarily focus on text, images, or sequences, the proposed model aims to bridge the gap by integrating information from diverse data types such as knowledge graphs, protein structures, and chemical molecules. By leveraging the strengths of large language models in capturing textual information and combining them with specialized encoders for biological sequences and graph structures, the foundation model holds immense potential to revolutionize various aspects of healthcare, including diagnosis, treatment planning, and drug discovery.\n\nThe model can be used to create an interactive agent that clinicians and researchers can utilize to help them navigate problems in biomedical research, thereby enhancing decision-making processes in clinical practice and computational biology research. For instance, the incorporation of knowledge graphs may allow for a more nuanced exploration of relationships between genes, drugs, and diseases, facilitating target identification for drug discovery as well as drug repurposing, which accelerates the clinical trial progress.\n\nMoreover, the integration of protein structure and chemical molecule data should enable our model to delve deeper into molecular mechanisms underlying diseases and drug interactions. [truncated]",
./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0211.stderr.log:552:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0065.stderr.log:1774:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0408.stderr.log:313:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_003257","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Abstract explicitly says 'In this review' and describes a 'systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis'. Biology and LLMs are both central.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this review, we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics...\""},{"record_id":"rec_003258","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title links knowledge graphs and protein structures to large language models, but the abstract is fragmentary and reads like a proposal ('Here I propose to work on...'). It is not clear whether this is a completed primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation model for biomedical graphs: Integrating knowledge graphs and protein structures to large language models\"; \"Here I propose to work on...\""},{"record_id":"rec_003259","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract tie LLMs to biological sequence analysis, but the abstract fragment is too incomplete to confirm whether this is a primary study, review, or resource paper. The biological and language components are still evident.","evidence_snippet":"\"Large language models for biological sequence analysis in infectious disease research\""},{"record_id":"rec_003260","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about multimodal oncology data integration and foundation-model-driven dataset generation, but it does not describe a substantive text or language component. That makes it outside the text-bio scope.","evidence_snippet":"\"Embedding-Based Deep Learning Frameworks for Multimodal Oncology Data Integration\"; \"foundation model-driven approach to generating multimodal oncology datasets\""},{"record_id":"rec_003261","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly says 'a survey' and the abstract says it 'comprehensively reviews' LLM applications in biomedical data analysis. This is a review rather than a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"The large language models on biomedical data analysis: a survey\"; \"This comprehensive review\""},{"record_id":"rec_003262","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract repeatedly frames the paper as a comprehensive review of LLMs in bioinformatics and 'systematically summarize[s] the current research status'. That is clearly review/editorial.","evidence_snippet":"\"This comprehensive review systematically examines...\""},{"record_id":"rec_003263","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title says 'A Survey of Multimodal Large Language Models in Biomedical Engineering and Healthcare', so this is a review paper rather than a primary model contribution.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Survey of Multimodal Large Language Models in Biomedical Engineering and Healthcare\""},{"record_id":"rec_003264","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a new foundational agent for multi-omics interpretation with explicit reasoning and latent embeddings, but it does not describe a text or language component. It is therefore outside the generative text-bio scope.","evidence_snippet":"\"We introduce BiOmics, a foundational agent that synthesizes multi-omics data\"; \"explicit reasoning space\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0211.stderr.log:553:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0065.stderr.log:3198:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0408.prompt.txt:51:[{"record_id":"rec_003257","title":"LLMAgent4Bio: LLM Agents for Biological Intelligence Across Genomics, Proteomics, Spatial Biology, and Biomedicine","abstract":"Abstract\n                Large language models are evolving from passive predictors into agentic systems capable of planning, tool use, and multimodal reasoning. This shift is especially consequential for biology, where complex, noisy, and multi-scale data require adaptive and integrative computational strategies. In this review, we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis, and automated bioinformatics workflows. We analyze more than fifty emerging systems and organize them within a unifying framework that characterizes agentic traits such as autonomous decision-making, external tool invocation, memory, and self-correction. Across domains, agentic LLMs show early promise in enabling multi-step analysis, linking heterogeneous evidence, and supporting exploratory scientific tasks. At the same time, our comparative assessment highlights consistent challenges, including unstable reasoning, limited biological grounding, retrieval misalignment, and barriers to reproducibility and biosafety. We conclude by outlining opportunities for trustworthy and collaborative biological agents, including multimodal integration, closedloop experimental design, and robust evaluation practices. This survey aims to clarify the emerging landscape and chart a path toward reliable agentic systems for biological discovery.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003258","title":"Foundation model for biomedical graphs: Integrating knowledge graphs and protein structures to large language models","abstract":", multimodal LLM in the biomedical domain is primarily limited to images, text, and/or sequence  data. Here I propose to work on multimodal LLM  -art foundation model with genetics and","doi":"","year":"2024","venue":"Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003259","title":"Large language models for biological sequence analysis in infectious disease research","abstract":"models, genomic language models, and multimodal models,  the emergence of computational,  LLM-based approaches for  foundation model and benchmark for multi species genomes","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"Biosafety and Health","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003260","title":"Embedding-Based Deep Learning Frameworks for Multimodal Oncology Data Integration","abstract":"our foundation model-driven approach to generating multimodal oncology datasets. We show   We discuss the challenges and limitations of implementing multimodal data-fusion models","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003261","title":"The large language models on biomedical data analysis: a survey","abstract":"of LLMs in genomics, proteomics, transcriptomics, single-cell  BERT-based LLM that uses a  multi-modal self-supervised pre foundation model and benchmark for multi-species genome.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"IEEE Journal of …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003262","title":"Bridging artificial intelligence and biological sciences: a comprehensive review of large language models in bioinformatics","abstract":"Abstract\n               Large language models (LLMs), representing a breakthrough advancement in artificial intelligence, have demonstrated substantial application value and development potential in bioinformatics research, particularly showing significant progress in the processing and analysis of complex biological data. This comprehensive review systematically examines the development and applications of LLMs in bioinformatics, with particular emphasis on their advancements in protein and nucleic acid structure prediction, omics analysis, drug design and screening, and biomedical literature mining. This work highlights the distinctive capabilities of LLMs in end-to-end learning and knowledge transfer paradigms. Additionally, this paper thoroughly discusses the major challenges confronting LLMs in current applications, including key issues such as model interpretability and data bias. Furthermore, this review comprehensively explores the potential of LLMs in cross-modal learning and interdisciplinary development. In conclusion, this paper aims to systematically summarize the current research status of LLMs in bioinformatics, objectively evaluate their advantages and limitations, and provide insights and recommendations for future research directions, thereby positioning LLMs as essential tools in bioinformatics research and fostering innovative developments in the biomedical field.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"Briefings in …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003263","title":"A Survey of Multimodal Large Language Models in Biomedical Engineering and Healthcare","abstract":"data are inherently multimodal: imaging, waveforms, genomics,  fine-tunes the Otter  foundation model to generate volumetric CT  Xu, “Do we really need a multimodal llm decoder?","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"Authorea …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003264","title":"BiOmics: A Foundational Agent for Grounded and Autonomous Multi-omics Interpretation","abstract":"Abstract\n                While AI has automated bioinformatic workflows, biological interpretation remains fragmented and often disconnected from mechanistic insights. Existing AI is bifurcated between statistical “black-box” models that lack logical grounding and simple agents restricted to shallow knowledge retrieval. To bridge this divide, we introduce BiOmics, a foundational agent that synthesizes multi-omics data with adaptive knowledge for biological interpretation. BiOmics introduces a novel dual-track architecture comprising a harmonized explicit reasoning space for grounded logic and a unified latent embedding space for high-dimensional association mapping. This architecture enables a transformative “Retrieving-Reasoning-Predicting” paradigm for purposeful, cross-scale inference traversing the biological hierarchy, from molecular variants to disease phenotypes. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that BiOmics surpasses state-of-the-art AI agents and specialized algorithms, markedly augmenting the granularity and depth of biological insights. Specifically, BiOmics exhibits unique superiority in uncovering indirect pathogenic variants, achieving reference-free cell annotation, and prioritizing drug repurposing candidates tailored to specific datasets. BiOmics further enriches the interpretive landscape of biological entities, leveraging its reasoning-grounded knowledge graph to uncover deep functional contexts. Ultimately, BiOmics provides a versatile engineering foundation to transition AI for Science from descriptive “data fitting” to autonomous, knowledge-driven interpretation.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]}]
./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0211.stderr.log:554:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0165.stderr.log:65:[{"record_id":"rec_003255","title":"Challenges in AI-driven biomedical multimodal data fusion and analysis","abstract":"Abstract\n                  The rapid development of biological and medical examination methods has vastly expanded personal biomedical information, including molecular, cellular, image, and electronic health record datasets. Integrating this wealth of information enables precise disease diagnosis, biomarker identification, and treatment design in clinical settings. Artificial intelligence (AI) techniques, particularly deep learning models, have been extensively employed in biomedical applications, demonstrating increased precision, efficiency, and generalization. The success of the large language and vision models further significantly extends their biomedical applications. However, challenges remain in learning these multimodal biomedical datasets, such as data privacy, fusion, and model interpretation. In this review, we provide a comprehensive overview of various biomedical data modalities, multimodal representation learning methods, and the applications of AI in biomedical data integrative analysis. Additionally, we discuss the challenges in applying these deep learning methods and how to better integrate them into biomedical scenarios. We then propose future directions for adapting deep learning methods with model pretraining and knowledge integration to advance biomedical research and benefit their clinical applications.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"Genomics …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003255","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is explicitly a review of biomedical multimodal data fusion and analysis. It mentions large language and vision models, but the abstract does not establish a clear text-bio candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"“In this review, we provide a comprehensive overview” and “the success of the large language and vision models”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003255","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI-driven biomedical multimodal data fusion and analysis. It discusses deep learning and large language and vision models generally, but does not describe a specific generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this review, we provide a comprehensive overview\"; \"the success of the large language and vision models\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003256","title":"Large language models for bioinformatics","abstract":"LLMs are a type of foundation model trained on vast datasets to  omics data (eg, genomics,  transcriptomics, proteomics) and  Recent advances in multi-modal LLM architecture have","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Quantitative …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003256","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and fragment suggest bioinformatics-focused LLM content, but the abstract is too truncated to determine whether this is a primary model paper or a review. The text-bio bridge is not clearly stated.","evidence_snippet":"“Large language models for bioinformatics” and “Recent advances in multi-modal LLM architecture”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003256","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about large language models in bioinformatics as a general topic, not a new candidate model. The foundation-model statement is definitional rather than evidence for a specific pretrained model.","evidence_snippet":"\"LLMs are a type of foundation model trained on vast datasets\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003257","title":"LLMAgent4Bio: LLM Agents for Biological Intelligence Across Genomics, Proteomics, Spatial Biology, and Biomedicine","abstract":"Abstract\n                Large language models are evolving from passive predictors into agentic systems capable of planning, tool use, and multimodal reasoning. This shift is especially consequential for biology, where complex, noisy, and multi-scale data require adaptive and integrative computational strategies. In this review, we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis, and automated bioinformatics workflows. We analyze more than fifty emerging systems and organize them within a unifying framework that characterizes agentic traits such as autonomous decision-making, external tool invocation, memory, and self-correction. Across domains, agentic LLMs show early promise in enabling multi-step analysis, linking heterogeneous evidence, and supporting exploratory scientific tasks. At the same time, our comparative assessment highlights consistent challenges, including unstable reasoning, limited biological grounding, retrieval misalignment, and barriers to reproducibility and biosafety. We conclude by outlining opportunities for trustworthy and collaborative biological agents, including multimodal integration, closedloop experimental design, and robust evaluation practices. This survey aims to clarify the emerging landscape and chart a path toward reliable agentic systems for biological discovery.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003257","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Abstract explicitly says 'In this review' and describes a 'systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis'. Biology and LLMs are both central.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this review, we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics...\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003257","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a systematic review of LLM-based agents, not a primary model paper. It summarizes existing systems across biology rather than presenting a new generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_r